# General Coding Guidelines

Prioritize clarity and maintainability. Code should read like a polished demo: obvious intent, best practices, no cleverness.

## Frontend: designer-made, readable, accessible

- Layouts need clear hierarchy, consistent spacing, and obvious visual grouping — not default browser chrome.
- Buttons must look and feel intentional: sufficient size, contrast, hover/focus/disabled states, and a visible focus ring.
- Backgrounds and surfaces must support readable text (contrast), not compete with content.
- Prefer semantic HTML and accessible names (`button`, `label`, `aria-*` only when native semantics are not enough).
- Interactive targets should be easy to scan and hit; avoid cramped, low-contrast, or unlabeled controls.

```html
<!-- BAD: tiny, low-contrast, no focus style -->
<div onclick="save()">ok</div>

<!-- GOOD: semantic, labeled, focusable, high contrast -->
<button type="submit" class="btn-primary">Save changes</button>
```

## Functions: clear, single-purpose, under 50 lines

- One function does one thing. Name it after that thing.
- Extract helpers instead of nesting unrelated steps.
- Prefer early returns over deep `if` pyramids.

```ts
// BAD: mixed concerns, hard to scan
function handleSubmit(form) { /* validate + api + toast + navigate... */ }

// GOOD: each step is a named unit
function handleSubmit(form: FormState) {
  const data = parseForm(form);
  const errors = validateOrder(data);
  if (errors) return showErrors(errors);
  return submitOrder(data);
}
```

## Files: under 300 lines

- Split by responsibility (UI vs data vs helpers), not by dumping more into the same file.
- Colocate closely related pieces; do not create kitchen-sink modules.

## Readability over cleverness

- Explicit names, straightforward control flow, no unexplained magic numbers or one-letter identifiers.
- Follow language/framework conventions already in the repo.
- If a change fights these limits, refactor first — do not grow functions or files "just this once."
